# Gotchas

> **This file is a stub.** The shape exists so you know where the content belongs before there is
> any. It is marked `stub` in `.sdlc/CONFIG.md`, and that state is load-bearing: agents must not
> cite a stub. A reviewer that needs a rule from here says *"no rule exists for this"* instead of
> citing a document that says nothing.
>
> **Its first entry belongs here when:** somebody loses an hour to something that made no sense at the time.
>
> When that happens, write the entry, delete this block, and change this doc's state in
> `.sdlc/CONFIG.md` to `real`. A stub that has quietly become real is a doc nobody is allowed to
> cite and everybody assumes they can.

Traps that cost real time, and that nothing else in the repo would have told you.

## How to add one

**Whoever loses the hour writes the entry, in the same change.** Not later, not in a ticket.

**Title it with the misdiagnosis, not the rule.** You reach for this file while believing something
wrong, so the heading has to match the wrong belief, not the correct one. "The database is
unreachable" finds the entry; "always pass the port explicitly" does not.

Then: what it looked like, what it actually was, and what to do. In that order.

An entry that only states the rule is a rule nobody needed.

## Traps that live with their subject

Some traps belong next to the thing they are about, not here — a second copy drifts. Route them,
and keep this table honest about where they went.

